A bizarre story is unfolding in Jammu and Kashmir.
In a Hindu-majority India, the Kashmiri Pandits, the minority in the Kashmir Valley, continue to face existential threats from Pakistan-sponsored terrorists, three decades after their exodus.
As India prepares to celebrate its 80th Independence Day, the occasion offers an opportunity to reflect on India’s long journey since it broke free from British colonial rule in 1947.
Yet, amid these celebrations, the painful reality haunts the Kashmiri Pandits, the indigenous Hindu community of Kashmir: Over 3 decades of separation from their homeland.
The irony is that India, one of the world’s biggest economies and military powers, has failed to provide one thing that every citizen of the country enjoys: security to the aborigines of the Kashmir Valley.
In 1989-90, amid escalating threats and targeted killings, thousands of Pandits fled the Kashmir Valley overnight to save their lives and dignity. It was one of the most painful episodes in Kashmir’s history.
Now, adding to the wounds of their painful past, at a time when New Delhi is attempting to resettle a few of them, fresh death threats from Pakistan-linked terrorists are jeopardizing the plan.
This time, a terrorist organization—the United Liberation Council (ULC), a shadow front of Lashkar-e-Taiba (LeT)—has issued threats against Pandit employees working under the Prime Minister’s Special Employment Package, circulating an online letter containing their names and phone numbers.
Reminiscent of 1990, when a similar strategy of public hit lists and targeted profiling was followed to instill terror, these fresh disclosures have once again shaken the community. Fearing for their safety, the J&K government administration has now directed them to work from home and granted administrative leave until August 25.
